  "mechanismSummary": "Hünig's base deprotonates Boc-alanine. The carboxylate adds to the amidinium carbon of HATU, and the triazole leaves as the OAt⁻ anion, giving a reactive O-acylisouronium ion. OAt⁻ adds to its acyl carbon and neutral tetramethylurea leaves: the OAt active ester. The amine adds to the ester, OAt⁻ leaves, and a proton transfer gives the dipeptide and HOAt. The pyridine nitrogen of OAt, next to the ester oxygen, holds the incoming amine by a hydrogen bond, so HATU couples fast with little racemisation. The amine is added after the acid has been activated: left with HATU, an amine can attack the amidinium carbon itself and be capped as a guanidine. The app does not check stereochemistry yet.",
